Z||000000||JC Webb||01-18-2001||07:21 PM||||What obstacles would I run into if I wanted to replace my park lights with, a similiar sized fog or driving light?  Could I use the park light wiring so that they still blinked?  I know that it is probably illegal to have a clear park light but my inspector won't care.  I was considering PIAA's.  Does anyone have any expierience with these lights?  Thanks!<P>John C. Webb||||reg||5
Z||000001||RaeQwan||01-19-2001||02:37 AM||||clear park lights arn't illegal.. metro's have em  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/raeqwansig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||01-19-2001||06:29 AM||||obvious ques......why? they would work, and blink too, but it definatly would need a heavy duty flasher relay, too much current draw for stock one. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||01-19-2001||01:49 PM||||Add the driving lights to the turn signal locations and then stick a set of small motor cycle turn signals in the recessed area of the grill.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex2004||01-19-2001||07:43 PM||||When you mean driving lights, do you mean "daytime running lights"?  cause if so, i want them on my car too! lmk...<P>Alex...||||reg||5
Z||000005||daved||01-19-2001||09:57 PM||||I remember Wallmart used to have split lights about the right size, around a third of which was amber.  Went back recently, and they are no longer selling them.  <BR>Anyone seen them around or knows who makes/sells them?  I'm sure there are a lot of us could use a solution to the running lights problem.||||reg||1
Z||000006||danny boy||01-19-2001||11:09 PM||||I did this. the lights I used are from Blazer<BR>which I got at Wal Mart(the split ones talked about earlier are too big) for $20. Instead of the drivivg lights doubling as turnsignals I used an extra set of side marker lights and mounted them in the bumper moulding.Keep in mind this was on an 86 coupe<BR>so this wont work on GT cars. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||01-19-2001||11:47 PM||||As far as using driving lights as DRL.... GM has a kit available. 2 actually. one kit is DRL and the other is DRL with "twilight sentinel." T.S. is sort of an auto on function. No, i don't know much about it.<P>You'd likely want the DRL only kit for use with a driving light.<P>No, you can't hook the driving lights up to the turn/park circuit without doing some planning. There is more involved than the lamp on the front of the car, or for that matter the signal can. study the Haynes book or something else with the schematics in it.<P>Clear lens parking lights usually use an Amber coated bulb in them. At least all the ones I've ever seen do. You could look metro up in the parts book to see, but I'm guessing it lists an amber lamp to.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||lawrence||01-20-2001||10:25 AM||||I sell a Daytime Running Light module that can be installed in your car.  It isn't a plug-in that goes into the fuse block, but a module that attaches directly to the turn signals.<P>It doesn't have "twilight sentinal", but they come on when you start the car, and go off when you turn on your lights.<P>If you are interested, click below.<P>Have fun,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/products" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/products</A>  - Product Page||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fiero_Freak||01-20-2001||04:06 PM||||   Could you possible still use some driving lights and drill a little hole in the side of the reflector to put a yellow bulb in from the side... so it would do both?  Just don't turn on the driving lights for inspection or right when the cop pulls you over.||||reg||1
